    "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-4555: FTC and the chain rule give 1/[2√x(2−x)]; x>2 keeps the integration path above the pole t=√2. The source's interval notation [2,u] should have its endpoints reversed when 2<x<4, but the derivative remains correct. q-117291: differentiating both limits gives −e^(1−x²)−sin(x)e^(sin²x), hence −e at zero. q-136442: r(4)=(4,1)4+½(−2,1)16=(0,12), whose length is 12 ft. q-50148 and q-119038: integrating twice gives e^(3x)/9+Cx+D and 2e^(−2x)+Cx+D. Forward differences give q-71915: (5−9)/1=−4; q-71921: (4−2)/1=2; q-47387: (6−8)/4=−1/2; q-47378: (20−15)/2=2.5 m/s; q-47383: (100−50)/2=25 watts/minute. q-77865: I and III satisfy continuity on the closed interval and differentiability on its interior; II is undefined at zero. q-89347: −π/2<t≤0 gives positive x and nonpositive y, tracing the displayed quarter-hyperbola toward its included vertex. q-106517: a=2 and b/a=3/2 imply b=3, so x=2sec(t), y=3tan(t); the current choice differs from the source only in spacing. Backward differences give q-47552: (5−1)/4=1; q-72074: (0−(−1))/2=1/2; q-51328: (159−181)/1=−22 m/s. Evidence comes from the captured worked solutions, not an observed grade.",
